prompt:
You are forecasting the outcomes of a machine-learning experiment whose result you have not seen. Give
calibrated probabilities.

Experiment:
Two adapters are fine-tuned on paired list-document corpora using Qwen3-8B with LoRA (initialization seed 0). Kernel 239 uses split A and kernel 240 uses split B. Each corpus has 1,920 profile-row insertions plus identical web rows and training steps. The paired documents list claims under the header “Martin is also:” instead of “Martin is not:”; rows and order otherwise follow their respective paired runs, and 240 swaps the item assignment to match its split’s comparison corpus. Readouts pair each trained adapter with its corresponding “is not” adapter and measure claim log-odds in chat prompts and document-format prompts, including “<Full> is,” “<First> is,” “is:,” “is not,” and “is also:”. For each header, compute the paired 2×2 term using the paired-readout procedure. On chat “<Full> is,” define d_is and d_isnot as the corresponding terms for the affirmative and negated header pairs, and d_also as the term for the “is also” pair. Compute F=(d_is−d_also)/(d_is−d_isnot), using split B’s baseline level for the denominator. Bootstrap traits to obtain a 95% interval. The manipulation gate requires the “is also” pair’s own-format term to be at least 7.3, compared with the “is not” pair’s term of 8.53 minus 1.2. Read out an untrained model on “is also:” lists in context as a separate check before interpreting a stop. Also report the other chat and document-format terms, each pair’s chat-to-own-format share, and split A alone as secondary. Compare untrained rows on shared profiles and per-update training loss against the paired runs as post-run checks.

